ediate report\"` or `\"final report\"`.\n\n## 2. `entity`: section 1, mandatory at every stage\n- **1.2** Name of the entity submitting the report\n- **1.3** Its identification code (its LEI, if it's a financial entity)\n- **1.4** Type of the affected financial entity: one or more values from the list in Article 2(1) of DORA, such as `credit institution` or `payment institution`\n- **1.7–1.9** Primary contact's name, email and telephone (`+49 69 …` format)\n- **1.10–1.12** Second contact's name, email and telephone\n- **1.15** Reporting currency (ISO 4217 code, e.g. `EUR`)\n- Only if they apply:\n  - **1.5/1.6** Name and LEI of the affected entity, if it isn't the one submitting\n  - **1.13/1.14** Name and LEI of the ultimate parent undertaking, if the entity is in a group\n\n## 3. `incident`: sections 2–4\n**Section 2, mandatory at every stage (this is all the initial notification needs):**\n- **2.1** Your own incident reference code. It never changes across the three reports.\n- **2.2** When the incident was detected, in UTC (`2026-03-02T06:40:00Z`)\n- **2.3** When it was classified as major, in UTC\n- **2.4** Plain-language description of what happened\n- **2.5** Classification criteria that were met: one or more of clients/counterparts/transactions affected, reputational impact, duration and service downtime, geographical spread, data losses, critical services affected, economic impact\n- **2.7** How it was discovered (from a fixed list: IT Security, staff, clients, monitoring systems, etc.)\n- **2.9** Whether the business continuity plan was activated (true/false)\n- Only if they apply:\n  - **2.6** Affected EEA countries, required if 2.5 includes geographical spread\n  - **2.8** Originating third-party provider or financial entity\n  - **2.10** Other relevant information\n\n**Section 3, added for the intermediate report:**\n- When the incident occurred\n- Numbers and percentages of clients and of financial counterparts affected\n- Whether those figures are actual or estimates\n- Duration\n- Critical services affected\n- Incident type(s)\n- Affected functional areas and infrastructure\n- Impact on clients' financial interests\n- Other authorities informed\n- Temporary actions taken\n\nSeveral more fields become mandatory depending on your answers. For example, choosing cybersecurity-related as the incident type requires threats/techniques and indicators of compromise.\n\n**Section 4, added for the final report:**\n- Root cause classification and details\n- Resolution summary\n- When the root cause was addressed and when the incident was resolved\n- Economic impact\n- Gross costs and losses\n- Financial recoveries\n\n## Running it\n```\nmetais validate report.metais --data <dir>\nmetais render report.metais --data <dir> --out <outdir>\n```\n`validate` lists every missing mandatory field and every badly formed value in one pass. The package only produces `incident-report.md`. You still have to submit it to your competent authority through the channel that authority prescribes.\n\nThere are worked examples for each stage. `metais ls report.metais examples/initial/ -r` lists the inputs and the expected output for the initial notification.\n\nIf you tell me which stage you're at and give me the incident details, I can create the data files and run the validation.","ttft_ms":2822,"type":"result","duration_ms":39433,"uuid":"712881cc-437c-4883-8c67-62f726fb805b","ttft_stream_ms":1743,"time_to_request_ms":94,"first_content_frame_ms":1743,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
